<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Marion</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.) · capital Marion</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Cypriot kingdom on the north-west coast that grew rich on copper trade with Athens until Ptolemy I destroyed it in 312 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Marion grew near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis-chrysochous">Polis Chrysochous</a> in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mines of Limni, in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. It emerged as a city-kingdom in the Cypro-Archaic period, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> across the sea.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Marion exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and imported large quantities of Athenian painted pottery, found in its cemeteries. Its kings, some with Greek names such as Stasioikos and others possibly Phoenician, issued coins in the 5th and 4th centuries BCE. Inscriptions use the Cypriot syllabary for Greek.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stasioikos-ii">Stasioikos II</a>, sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I</a>, who destroyed Marion in 312 BCE and moved its people to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-paphos">Paphos</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II</a> refounded the site as Arsinoe c. 270 BCE, and the modern town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polis">Polis</a> stands there today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-marion.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Marion" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Badseed, using work by Evil berry and Zamonin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_kingdoms_of_Cyprus_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 312 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Marion</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Stasioikos II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cypriot Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arcado-Cypriot Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marion, Arsinoe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-656&amp;focus=kingdom-of-marion" aria-label="Kingdom of Marion on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5502 910 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Marion lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5822 1166L6140 1251M5822 1166L5772 1297"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5772 1297h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5822 1166h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5822 1166h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-656&amp;focus=kingdom-of-marion">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

